{\pair{\boldsymbol{x}}{\boldsymbol{s}_n^*}=\eta_n}$. Thus,
$\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}$ is the solution of the problem 
\begin{equation}
\label{evb8uh4505a}
\minimize{\pair{\boldsymbol{p}}{\boldsymbol{s}_n^*}=\eta_n}
{\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{p})-\pair{\boldsymbol{p}}{\nabla
\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_n)}}
\end{equation}
which, using standard first order conditions, is characterized by
(see also \cite[Remark~6.13]{BB97} and 
\cite[Lemma~2.2.1]{Cens97}) 
\begin{equation}
\label{evb8uh4505b}
\begin{cases}
\nabla\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2})=\nabla
\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_n)-\lambda\boldsymbol{s}_n^*\\
\pair{\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}}{\boldsymbol{s}_n^*}=\eta_n\\
\lambda\in\ensuremath{\left]0,+\infty\right[}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
In view of \cite[Theorem~5.10]{Ccm01}, the Lagrange multiplier 
$\lambda$ is uniquely determined by the equation 
$\pair{\nabla\boldsymbol{f}^*(\nabla\boldsymbol{f}
(\boldsymbol{x}_n)-\lambda\boldsymbol{s}_n^*)}
{\boldsymbol{s}_n^*}=\eta_n$. The problem therefore reduces to
finding the solution $\overline{\lambda}$ to this equation in $\ensuremath{\left]0,+\infty\right[}$
and then setting $\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}=\nabla\boldsymbol{f}^*(
\nabla\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_n)-\overline{\lambda}
\boldsymbol{s}_n^*)$. Likewise, it follows from \eqref{e:Q} that 
$\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1}$ is the unique solution to the problem 
\begin{equation}
\label{evb8uh4505c}
\minimize{\substack{\pair{\boldsymbol{p}-\boldsymbol{x}_n}
{\nabla\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_0)-
\nabla\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_n)}\ensuremath{\leqslant} 0\\
\pair{\boldsymbol{p}-\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}}
{\nabla\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n})-
\nabla\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2})}\ensuremath{\leqslant} 0}}
{\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{p})-\pair{\boldsymbol{p}}{\nabla
\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_0)}}.
\end{equation}
Depending on the number of active constraints, this 
problem boils down to determining 
up to two Lagrange multipliers in $\ensuremath{\left]0,+\infty\right[}$.

In Problem~\ref{IVpb:V3}, suppose that, for every 
$i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}$ and every $k\in\{1,\ldots,p\}$, 
$\varphi_i$ and $\psi_k$ are supercoercive 
Legendre functions satisfying Condition~\ref{IVC:1}, 
that $\nabla\varphi_i$ and $\nabla\psi_k$ are uniformly 
continuous on bounded subset of $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int}\,}\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,}\varphi_i$ 
and $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int}\,}\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,}\psi_k$, respectively, and that
$\sum_{i=1}^mL_{ki}(\ensuremath{\text{\rm int}\,}\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,}\varphi_i)\subset\ensuremath{\text{\rm int}\,}\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,}\psi_k$. 
Then, in Proposition~\ref{4p:1}, we can choose, for every 
$i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}$ and every $k\in\{1,\ldots,p\}$, 
$h_i=\varphi_i$ and $j_k=\psi_k$, and in \eqref{4eKKnhf74g203}, 
we obtain
\begin{equation}
\label{4ekIuh729a}
a_{i,n}=\nabla h_i^*\left(\frac{\nabla h_i(x_{i,n})
-\gamma_n\ensuremath{\displaystyle\sum}_{k=1}^pL_{ki}^*y_{k,n}^*}{1+\gamma_n}\right)
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
b_{k,n}=\nabla j_k^*\left(\frac{\nabla j_k\bigg(\ensuremath{\displaystyle\sum}_{i=1}^m
L_{ki}x_{i,n}\bigg)+\mu_ny_{k,n}^*}{1+\mu_n}\right).
\end{equation}
For example, suppose that, for every $i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}$ 
and every $k\in\{1,\ldots,p\}$, $\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}_i=\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$, 
$\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}_k=\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$, and $\varphi_i=h_i$ is the Hellinger-like 
function, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
\varphi_i\colon\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}\to\ensuremath{\left]-\infty,+\infty\right]}\colon x_i\mapsto
\begin{cases}
-\sqrt{1-x_i^2},&\text{if}\;\;
x_i\in\left[-1,1\right];\\
\ensuremath{{+\infty}},&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Then \eqref{4ekIuh729a} becomes
\begin{equation}
\label{4ekIuh729b}
a_{i,n}=\frac{x_{i,n}-\gamma_n
\bigg(\ensuremath{\displaystyle\sum}_{k=1}^pL_{ki}^*y_{k,n}^*\bigg)\sqrt{1-x_{i,n}^2}}
{\sqrt{(1+\gamma_n)^2(1-x_{i,n}^2)+\bigg(x_{i,n}
-\gamma_n\bigg(\ensuremath{\displaystyle\sum}_{k=1}^pL_{ki}^*y_{k,n}^*\bigg)
\sqrt{1-x_{i,n}^2}\bigg)^2}}.
\end{equation}
Furthermore, as shown in the next section, in 
finite-dimensional spaces, we can remove Condition~\ref{IVC:1} 
and the assumption on the uniform continuity of 
$(\nabla\varphi_i)_{1\ensuremath{\leqslant} i\ensuremath{\leqslant} m}$ and 
$(\nabla\psi_k)_{1\ensuremath{\leqslant} k\ensuremath{\leqslant} p}$.

The approach we present goes back to Haugazeau's algorithm 
\cite[Th\'eor\`eme~3-2]{Haug68} (see also
\cite[Theorem~29.3]{Livre1}) for projecting a point onto 
the intersection of closed convex sets in a Hilbert space using 
the projections onto the individual sets. The method was 
extended in \cite{Sico00} to minimize certain convex functions 
over the intersection of closed convex sets in Banach spaces. 
The adaptation to the problem of finding the best Bregman
approximation from a closed convex set was investigated in
\cite{Pams03}.

\begin{definition}
\label{d:B-haugazeau}
{\rm\cite[Definition~3.1]{Sico03} and \cite[Section~3]{Pams03}}
Let $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathcal X}}$ be a reflexive real Banach space, let 
$\boldsymbol{f}\in\Gamma_0(\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathcal X}})$ be a Legendre function, let 
$\boldsymbol{x}_0\in\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,}\boldsymbol{f}$, let 
$\boldsymbol{x}\in\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,}\boldsymbol{f}$, and let 
$\boldsymbol{y}\in\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,}\boldsymbol{f}$. Then
\begin{align}
\label{eKKnhf74g19a}
H^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{y})
&=\menge{\boldsymbol{z}\in\boldsymbol{\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}}}{\pair{\boldsymbol{z}
-\boldsymbol{y}}{\nabla\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x})
-\nabla\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{y})}\ensuremath{\leqslant} 0}\nonumber\\
&=\menge{\boldsymbol{z}\in\boldsymbol{\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}}}{
D^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{z},\boldsymbol{y})+
D^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{y},\boldsymbol{x})\ensuremath{\leqslant}
D^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{z},\boldsymbol{x})}
\end{align}
is the closed affine half-space onto which $\boldsymbol{y}$ 
is the Bregman projection of $\boldsymbol{x}$ if
$\boldsymbol{x}\neq \boldsymbol{y}$. 
Moreover, if $H^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_0,\boldsymbol{x})
\cap H^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{y})
\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,}\boldsymbol{f}\neq\ensuremath{{\varnothing}}$, then
\begin{equation}
\label{e:Q}
Q^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_0,\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{y})
=P_{H^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_0,\boldsymbol{x})\cap 
H^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{y})}^{\boldsymbol{f}}
\boldsymbol{x}_0.
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma} {\rm\cite[Lemma~3.2]{Sico03}}
\label{l:2002}
Let $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathcal X}}$ be a reflexive real Banach space, and let 
$\boldsymbol{C}_1$ and $\boldsymbol{C}_2$ be convex subsets
of $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathcal X}}$ such that $\boldsymbol{C}_1$ is closed and 
$\boldsymbol{C}_1\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int}\,}\boldsymbol{C}_2\neq\ensuremath{{\varnothing}}$. Then 
$\overline{\boldsymbol{C}_1\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int}\,}\boldsymbol{C}}_2=
\boldsymbol{C}_1\cap\overline{\boldsymbol{C}}_2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proposition}
\label{p:2}
Let $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathcal X}}$ be a reflexive real Banach space, let 
$\boldsymbol{f}\in\Gamma_0(\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathcal X}})$ be a Legendre 
function, let $\boldsymbol{C}$ be a closed convex subset 
of $\overline{\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,}}\boldsymbol{f}$ such that 
$\boldsymbol{C}\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,}\boldsymbol{f}\neq\ensuremath{{\varnothing}}$,
let $\boldsymbol{x}_0\in\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,}\boldsymbol{f}$, and set 
$\overline{\boldsymbol{x}}=P_{\boldsymbol{C}}^{\boldsymbol{f}}
\boldsymbol{x}_0$. 
At every iteration $n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}$, find 
$\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}\in\ensuremath{\text{\rm int}\,}\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,}\boldsymbol{f}$ such that 
$\boldsymbol{C}\subset H^{\boldsymbol{f}}
(\boldsymbol{x}_n,\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2})$ and set
\begin{equation}
\label{e:haugazeau}
\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1}=Q^{\boldsymbol{f}}
\big(\boldsymbol{x}_0,\boldsymbol{x}_n,\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}\big).
\end{equation}
Then the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item
\label{p:2i-}
$(\forall n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N})$ $\boldsymbol{C}\subset H^{\boldsymbol{f}}
(\boldsymbol{x}_0,\boldsymbol{x}_n)$.
\item
\label{p:2i}
$(\boldsymbol{x}_n)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}$ is a well-defined bounded sequence 
in $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,}\boldsymbol{f}$.
\item
\label{p:2ii+} 
Suppose that, for some $n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}$, 
$\boldsymbol{x}_n\in\boldsymbol{C}$. Then $(\forall k\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N})$ 
$\boldsymbol{x}_{n+k}=\overline{\boldsymbol{x}}$.
\item
\label{p:2ii} 
$\sum_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}D^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1},
\boldsymbol{x}_n)<+\infty$.
\item
\label{p:2iii-} 
$(\forall n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N})$ $D^{\boldsymbol{f}}
(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2},\boldsymbol{x}_n)\ensuremath{\leqslant} 
D^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1},\boldsymbol{x}_n)$.
\item
\label{p:2iii} 
$\sum_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}D^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2},
\boldsymbol{x}_n)<+\infty$.
\item
\label{p:2iv} 
$[\,\boldsymbol{x}_n\ensuremath{\:\rightharpoonup\:}\overline{\boldsymbol{x}}$ and 
$\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_n)\to\boldsymbol{f}
(\overline{\boldsymbol{x}})\,]$ 
$\Leftrightarrow$ $D^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_n,
\overline{\boldsymbol{x}})\to 0$ 
$\Leftrightarrow$
$\ensuremath{{\mathfrak W}}(\boldsymbol{x}_n)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}\subset\boldsymbol{C}$. 
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Item~\ref{p:2i-} is found in 
\cite[Proof of Proposition~3.3]{Pams03}.
The first equivalence in \ref{p:2iv} follows from
\cite[Propositions~2.2(ii)]{Pams03}. To establish the remaining
assertions, set
\begin{equation}
\label{epOlk28ha21a}
(\forall n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N})\quad T_n=
P_{H^{\boldsymbol{f}}
(\boldsymbol{x}_n,\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2})}^{\boldsymbol{f}}.
\end{equation}
Then, for every $n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}$, Definition~\ref{d:B-haugazeau}
yields
$T_n\boldsymbol{x}_n=\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}$, 
\cite[Proposition~3.32(ii)(b)]{Sico03} yields
$\ensuremath{\text{\rm Fix}\,} T_n=H^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_n,\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2})
\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\:dom}\boldsymbol{f}}$, and we derive from Lemma~\ref{l:2002} that
\begin{equation}
\label{epOlk28ha21b}
\boldsymbol{C}\subset H^{\boldsymbol{f}}
(\boldsymbol{x}_n,\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2})
\cap\overline{\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,}}\boldsymbol{f}=
\overline{H^{\boldsymbol{f}}
(\boldsymbol{x}_n,\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2})\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\:dom}\boldsymbol{f}}}
=\overline{\ensuremath{\text{\rm Fix}\,}}T_n.
\end{equation}
On the other hand,
\begin{equation}
\label{epOlk28ha21c1}
(\forall n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N})\quad\ensuremath{{\varnothing}}\neq \boldsymbol{C}\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,}
\boldsymbol{f}\subset
H^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_n,\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2})
\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,}\boldsymbol{f}=\ensuremath{\text{\rm Fix}\,} T_n
\end{equation}
and, therefore, $\bigcap_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}\ensuremath{\text{\rm Fix}\,} T_n\neq\ensuremath{{\varnothing}}$.
Altogether, \cite[Condition~3.2]{Pams03} is satisfied
and it follows from \cite[Propositions~3.3 and 3.4]{Pams03} 
and \cite[Proof of Proposition~3.4(vii)]{Pams03} 
that the proof is complete.
\end{proof}

\label{IVsec:V}
Proposition~\ref{ppOlk28ha12}\ref{ppOlk28ha12ia} asserts that 
Problem~\ref{prob:1} reduces to finding the Bregman projection 
of a reference point $(x_0,y_0^*)$ onto the closed convex subset
$\boldsymbol{C}=\boldsymbol{Z}\cap\overline{\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,}}\boldsymbol{f}$
of $\overline{\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,}}\boldsymbol{f}$. Our strategy is to employ 
Proposition~\ref{p:2} for this task.
The following condition will be used subsequently
(see \cite[Examples~4.10, 5.11, and 5.13]{Sico03} for special cases).

\begin{condition}
{\rm\cite[Condition~4.3(ii)]{Pams03}}
\label{IVC:1}
Let $\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}$ be a reflexive real Banach space and let
$h\in\Gamma_0(\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}})$ be G\^ateaux differentiable on 
$\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} h\neq\ensuremath{{\varnothing}}$. For every sequence 
$(x_n)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}$ in $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} h$ and every bounded sequence 
$(y_n)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}$ in $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} h$, 
\begin{equation}
\label{IVbcn5y713b}
D^h(x_n,y_n)\to 0\quad\Rightarrow\quad x_n-y_n\to 0.
\end{equation}
\end{condition}

We now derive from Proposition~\ref{p:2} our best Bregman
approximation algorithm to solve Problem~\ref{prob:1}.

\begin{theorem}
\label{IVt:1}
Consider the setting of Problem~\ref{prob:1}. Let 
$h\in\Gamma_0(\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}})$ and $j\in\Gamma_0(\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}})$ be Legendre 
functions such that $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} f\subset\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} h$, 
$L(\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} f)\subset\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} j$, and there exist
$\varepsilon$ and $\delta$ in $\ensuremath{\left]0,+\infty\right[}$ such that 
$\nabla h+\varepsilon A$ and $\nabla j+\delta B$ are coercive. 
Let $\sigma\in\left[\max\{\varepsilon,\delta\},\ensuremath{{+\infty}}\right[$ 
and iterate
\begin{equation}
\label{IVe:Bfg+uh4f9-09h}
\begin{array}{l}
\text{for}\;n=0,1,\ldots\\
\left\lfloor
\begin{array}{l}
(\gamma_n,\mu_n)\in\left[\varepsilon,\sigma\right]
\times\left[\delta,\sigma\right]\\[1mm]
a_n=(\nabla h+\gamma_nA)^{-1}\big(\nabla h(x_n)-\gamma_n 
L^*y_n^*\big)\\[1mm]
a_n^*=\gamma_n^{-1}\big(\nabla h(x_n)-\nabla h(a_n)\big)-L^*y_n^*
\\[1mm]
b_n=(\nabla j+\mu_nB)^{-1}\big(\nabla j(Lx_n)+\mu_n y_n^*\big)
\\[1mm]
b_n^*=\mu_n^{-1}\big(\nabla j(Lx_n)-\nabla j(b_n)\big)+y_n^*
\\[1mm]
\boldsymbol{H}_n=
\menge{(x,y^*)\in\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathcal X}}}{\pair{x}{a_n^*+L^*b_n^*}
+\pair{b_n-La_n}{y^*}\ensuremath{\leqslant}\pair{a_n}{a_n^*}+\pair{b_n}{b_n^*}}\\
(x_{n+1/2},y_{n+1/2}^*)=
P_{\boldsymbol{H}_n}^{\boldsymbol{f}}
(x_n,y_n^*)\\
(x_{n+1},y_{n+1}^*)=Q^{\boldsymbol{f}}
\big((x_0,y_0^*),(x_n,y_n^*),(x_{n+1/2},y_{n+1/2}^*)\big).
\end{array}
\right.\\
\end{array}
\end{equation}
Then the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item
\label{IVt:1i} 
Let $n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}$. Then the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item
\label{IVt:1ia} 
$(x_n,y_n^*)=(\overline{x},\overline{y}^*)$.
\item
\label{IVt:1ib} 
$(x_n,y_n^*)\in\boldsymbol{Z}$.
\item
\label{IVt:1ic} 
$(x_n,y_n^*)\in\boldsymbol{H}_n$.
\item
\label{IVt:1id} 
$x_n=a_n$ and $y_n^*=b_n^*$.
\item
\label{IVt:1ie} 
$La_n=b_n$ and $a_n^*=-L^*b_n^*$.
\item
\label{IVt:1if} 
$\boldsymbol{H}_n=\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathcal X}}$.
\item
\label{IVt:1ig} 
$(x_{n+1/2},y_{n+1/2}^*)=(x_n,y_n^*)$.
\item
\label{IVt:1ih} 
$(x_{n+1},y_{n+1}^*)=(x_n,y_n^*)$.
\end{enumerate}
\item
\label{IVt:1ii} 
$\sum_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}D^f(x_{n+1},x_n)<\ensuremath{{+\infty}}$ and 
$\sum_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}D^{g^*}(y_{n+1}^*,y_n^*)<\ensuremath{{+\infty}}$.
\item
\label{IVt:1iii} 
$\sum_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}D^f(x_{n+1/2},x_n)<\ensuremath{{+\infty}}$ and 
$\sum_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}D^{g^*}(y_{n+1/2}^*,y_n^*)<\ensuremath{{+\infty}}$.
\item
\label{IVt:1iv}
Suppose that $f$, $g^*$, $h$, and $j$ satisfy Condition~\ref{IVC:1},
and that $\nabla h$ and $\nabla j$ are uniformly continuous on 
every bounded subset of $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} h$ and $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} j$, respectively.
Then $x_n\to\overline{x}$ and $y_n^*\to\overline{y}^*$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We apply Proposition~\ref{p:2} to 
\begin{equation}
\label{4e:C}
\boldsymbol{C}=\boldsymbol{Z}\cap\overline{\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,}}\boldsymbol{f}.
\end{equation}
It follows from Proposition~\ref{ppOlk28ha12}\ref{ppOlk28ha12ia}
and our assumptions that $\boldsymbol{C}$ is a closed convex 
subset of $\overline{\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,}}\boldsymbol{f}$ and that
$\boldsymbol{C}\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,}\boldsymbol{f}\neq\ensuremath{{\varnothing}}$. 
Moreover, Proposition~\ref{pvb8uh4504}\ref{pvb8uh4504i}
asserts that $\boldsymbol{f}$ is a Legendre function.
Now let $\gamma\in[\varepsilon,\ensuremath{{+\infty}}[$ and let 
$\mu\in[\delta,\ensuremath{{+\infty}}[$.
Since $h$ is strictly convex, $\nabla h$ is 
strictly monotone \cite[Theorem~2.4.4(ii)]{IVZali02} and 
$\nabla h+\gamma A$ is likewise. Let $(x^*,x_1)$ and 
$(x^*,x_2)$ be two elements in $\ensuremath{\text{\rm gra}\,}(\nabla h+\gamma A)^{-1}$ 
such that $x_1\not=x_2$. Then $(x_1,x^*)$ and $(x_2,x^*)$ lie in 
$\ensuremath{\text{\rm gra}\,}(\nabla h+\gamma A)$ and the strict monotonicity of 
$\nabla h+\gamma A$ implies that 
\begin{equation}
0=\pair{x_1-x_2}{x^*-x^*}>0,
\end{equation}
which is impossible. Thus,
\begin{equation}
\label{4e:single1}
(\nabla h+\gamma A)^{-1}\;\text{is at most single-valued}.
\end{equation} 
The same argument shows that
\begin{equation}
\label{4e:single2}
(\nabla j+\mu B)^{-1}\;\text{is at most single-valued}.
\end{equation} 
On the other hand, by assumption, there exists
$(x,y^*)\in\boldsymbol{Z}\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,}\boldsymbol{f}$.
It follows from \eqref{IVe:VKT} that $x\in\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,} A$ and 
$Lx\in\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,} B$. Furthermore, \eqref{ebcn5y731} yields 
$x\in\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} f$. Therefore
\begin{equation}
\label{ehHg7yG10a}
\begin{cases}
x\in\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,} A\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} f\subset\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,} A\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} h\\
Lx\in\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,} B\cap L(\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} f)\subset\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,} B\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} j.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Thus, $\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,} A\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} h\neq\ensuremath{{\varnothing}}$ and
$\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,} B\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} j\neq\ensuremath{{\varnothing}}$. It therefore follows from
Lemma~\ref{IVlhHg7yG02} that
\begin{equation}
\label{4e:single3}
\nabla h+\gamma A=(\nabla h+\varepsilon A)+(\gamma-\varepsilon)A 
\quad\text{and}\quad
\nabla j+\mu B=(\nabla j+\delta B)+(\mu-\delta)B
\;\;\text{are coercive}.
\end{equation}
Altogether, \eqref{4e:single1}, \eqref{4e:single2}, 
\eqref{4e:single3}, and Proposition~\ref{IVpp:20a} assert 
that the operators
\begin{equation}
\label{4:e12}
\begin{cases}
(\nabla h+\gamma A)^{-1}\colon\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}^*\to\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,} A\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} h\\
(\nabla j+\mu B)^{-1}\colon\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}^*\to\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,} B\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} j
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
are well defined and single-valued. Now set
\begin{equation}
\label{eKKnhf74g05}
(\forall n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N})\quad
\boldsymbol{x}_n=(x_n,y_n^*)\quad\text{and}\quad
\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}=(x_{n+1/2},y_{n+1/2}^*).
\end{equation}
Since \eqref{IVe:Bfg+uh4f9-09h} yields
\begin{equation}
\label{eKKnhf74g04}
(\forall n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N})\quad
(a_n,a_n^*)\in\ensuremath{\text{\rm gra}\,} A\quad\text{and}\quad (b_n,b_n^*)\in\ensuremath{\text{\rm gra}\,} B,
\end{equation}
it follows from \eqref{4e:C}, 
Proposition~\ref{ppOlk28ha12}\ref{ppOlk28ha12iib},
\eqref{IVe:Bfg+uh4f9-09h}, and 
Definition~\ref{d:B-haugazeau} that
\begin{equation}
\label{eKKnhf74g03}
(\forall n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N})\quad
\ensuremath{{\varnothing}}\neq\boldsymbol{C}\subset\boldsymbol{Z}\subset\boldsymbol{H}_n
=H^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_n,\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}).
\end{equation}
Hence, appealing to 
Proposition~\ref{pvb8uh4504}\ref{pvb8uh4504i} and 
\eqref{e:2001}, we see that
\begin{equation}
\label{IVe:32a}
(\forall n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N})\quad
P_{\boldsymbol{H}_n}^{\boldsymbol{f}}
\colon\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,}\boldsymbol{f}\to
\boldsymbol{H}_n\cap\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,}\boldsymbol{f}
\end{equation}
and that
\begin{equation}
\label{eKKnhf74g04i}
(\forall n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N})\quad
\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1}=Q^{\boldsymbol{f}}
\big(\boldsymbol{x}_0,\boldsymbol{x}_n,\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}\big).
\end{equation}
Thus, we derive from 
\eqref{eKKnhf74g05}, \eqref{eKKnhf74g03}, and 
Proposition~\ref{p:2}\ref{p:2i} that
$(x_n)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}$ and $(y_n^*)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}$ are well-defined
sequences in $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} f$ and $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} g^*$, respectively.

\ref{IVt:1i}: We prove the following implications.

\ref{IVt:1ia}$\Rightarrow$\ref{IVt:1ib}: Clear.

\ref{IVt:1ib}$\Rightarrow$\ref{IVt:1ia}: 
Proposition~\ref{p:2}\ref{p:2ii+}.

\ref{IVt:1ib}$\Rightarrow$\ref{IVt:1ic}: Clear by
\eqref{eKKnhf74g03}.

\ref{IVt:1ic}$\Rightarrow$\ref{IVt:1id}: In view of 
\eqref{IVe:Bfg+uh4f9-09h},
\begin{align}
0
&\ensuremath{\geqslant}\pair{x_n}{a_n^*+L^*b_n^*}
+\pair{b_n-La_n}{y_n^*}-\pair{a_n}{a_n^*}-\pair{b_n}{b_n^*}
\nonumber\\
&=\pair{x_n-a_n}{a_n^*+L^*y_n^*}+\pair{Lx_n-b_n}{b_n^*-y_n^*}
\nonumber\\
&=\gamma_n^{-1}\pair{x_n-a_n}{\nabla h(x_n)-\nabla h(a_n)}
+\mu_n^{-1}\pair{Lx_n-b_n}{\nabla j(Lx_n)-\nabla j(b_n)}.
\end{align}
Consequently, the strict monotonicity of $\nabla h$ and $\nabla j$ 
yields
\begin{equation}
x_n=a_n\quad\text{and}\quad Lx_n=b_n.
\end{equation}
Furthermore,
\begin{equation}
b_n^*=\mu_n^{-1}\big(\nabla j(Lx_n)-\nabla j(b_n)\big)+y_n^*
=\mu_n^{-1}\big(\nabla j(b_n)-\nabla j(b_n)\big)+y_n^*
=y_n^*.
\end{equation}

\ref{IVt:1id}$\Rightarrow$\ref{IVt:1ie}: We derive from 
\eqref{IVe:Bfg+uh4f9-09h} that
$a_n^*=\gamma_n^{-1}(\nabla h(x_n)-\nabla h(a_n))-L^*y_n^*
=-L^*y_n^*=-L^*b_n^*$. On the other hand, since
\begin{align}
\label{IWeQA8h33-30a}
\pair{La_n-b_n}{\nabla j(La_n)-\nabla j(b_n)}
&=\pair{Lx_n-b_n}{\nabla j(Lx_n)-\nabla j(b_n)}\nonumber\\
&=\mu_n\pair{Lx_n-b_n}{b_n^*-y_n^*}\nonumber\\
&=0,
\end{align}
the strict monotonicity of $\nabla j$ yields $La_n=b_n$.

\ref{IVt:1ie}$\Leftrightarrow$\ref{IVt:1if}: 
Proposition~\ref{ppOlk28ha12}\ref{ppOlk28ha12iia}.

\ref{IVt:1if}$\Rightarrow$\ref{IVt:1ig}: Indeed,
$\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}=P_{\boldsymbol{H}_n}^{\boldsymbol{f}}
\boldsymbol{x}_n=\boldsymbol{x}_n$.

\ref{IVt:1ig}$\Rightarrow$\ref{IVt:1ih}: We have
\begin{equation}
\label{IWeQA8h33-30b}
\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1}=Q^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_0,
\boldsymbol{x}_n,\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2})=
P^{\boldsymbol{f}}_{H^{\boldsymbol{f}}
(\boldsymbol{x}_0,\boldsymbol{x}_n)\cap H^{\boldsymbol{f}}
(\boldsymbol{x}_n,\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2})}
\boldsymbol{x}_0=P^{\boldsymbol{f}}_{H^{\boldsymbol{f}}
(\boldsymbol{x}_0,\boldsymbol{x}_n)}\boldsymbol{x}_0
=\boldsymbol{x}_n.
\end{equation}

\ref{IVt:1ih}$\Rightarrow$\ref{IVt:1ig}:  By
Proposition~\ref{p:2}\ref{p:2iii-},
$0\ensuremath{\leqslant} D^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2},\boldsymbol{x}_n)
\ensuremath{\leqslant} D^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1},\boldsymbol{x}_n)=0$.
Therefore 
$D^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2},\boldsymbol{x}_n)=0$
and we derive from \cite[Lemma~7.3(vi)]{Ccm01}
that $\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}=\boldsymbol{x}_n$.

\ref{IVt:1ig}$\Rightarrow$\ref{IVt:1ic}: Indeed,
$\boldsymbol{x}_n=\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}=
P^{\boldsymbol{f}}_{\boldsymbol{H}_n}
\boldsymbol{x}_n\in\boldsymbol{H}_n$.

\ref{IVt:1id}$\Rightarrow$\ref{IVt:1ib}: 
We derive from \eqref{IVe:Bfg+uh4f9-09h} that
\begin{equation}
\label{IWeQA8h33-30c}
\nabla h(x_n)-\gamma_n L^*y_n^*\in\nabla h(a_n)+\gamma_n Aa_n
=\nabla h(x_n)+\gamma_n Ax_n. 
\end{equation}
Hence $-L^*y_n^*\in Ax_n$. Likewise, as in 
\eqref{IWeQA8h33-30a}, we first obtain $Lx_n=b_n$ and then
\begin{equation}
\label{IWeQA8h33-30d}
\nabla j(Lx_n)+\mu_n y_n^*\in\nabla j(b_n)+\mu_n Bb_n
=\nabla j(Lx_n)+\mu_n B(Lx_n).
\end{equation}
Thus, $y_n^*\in B(Lx_n)$, i.e., $Lx_n\in B^{-1}y_n^*$.
In view of \eqref{IVe:VKT}, the implication is proved.

\ref{IVt:1ii}: 
Proposition~\ref{p:2}\ref{p:2ii} yields
\begin{equation}
\label{IVe:2312b}
\sum_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}D^f(x_{n+1},x_n)+\sum_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}D^{g^*}(y_{n+1}^*,y_n^*)
=\sum_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}D^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1},
\boldsymbol{x}_n)<\ensuremath{{+\infty}}.
\end{equation}

\ref{IVt:1iii}: Proposition~\ref{p:2}\ref{p:2iii} yields 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}D^f(x_{n+1/2},x_n)
+\sum_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}D^{g^*}(y_{n+1/2}^*,y_n^*)=
\sum_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}D^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2},
\boldsymbol{x}_n)<\ensuremath{{+\infty}}.
\end{equation}

\ref{IVt:1iv}: 
Proposition~\ref{p:2}\ref{p:2i} implies that
$(\boldsymbol{x}_n)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}$ is a bounded sequence 
in $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,}\boldsymbol{f}$. In turn, 
\begin{equation}
\label{eKKnhf74g15a}
(x_n)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}\in(\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} f)^{\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}\quad\text{and}\quad
(y^*_n)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}\in(\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} g^*)^{\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}\quad\text{are bounded}.
\end{equation}
On the other hand, by \eqref{IVe:Bfg+uh4f9-09h},
\begin{equation}
\label{IWeQA8h33-31a}
(\forall n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N})\quad
(x_{n+1/2},y_{n+1/2}^*)=
\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}=P_{\boldsymbol{H_n}}^{\boldsymbol{f}}
\boldsymbol{x}_n\in\boldsymbol{H}_n
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\label{IVe:IV8}
(\forall n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N})\quad\pair{x_{n+1/2}}{a_n^*+L^*b_n^*}
+\pair{b_n-La_n}{y_{n+1/2}^*}
=\pair{a_n}{a_n^*}+\pair{b_n}{b_n^*}.
\end{equation}
Therefore,
\begin{align}
\label{IVe:8re84}
(\forall n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N})\quad
&\|x_n-x_{n+1/2}\|\,\|a_n^*+L^*b_n^*\|
+\|b_n-La_n\|\,\|y_n^*-y_{n+1/2}^*\|\nonumber\\
&\ensuremath{\geqslant}\pair{x_n-x_{n+1/2}}{a_n^*+L^*b_n^*}
+\pair{b_n-La_n}{y_n^*-y_{n+1/2}^*}\nonumber\\
&=\pair{x_n}{a_n^*+L^*b_n^*}
+\pair{b_n-La_n}{y_n^*}
-\pair{a_n}{a_n^*}-\pair{b_n}{b_n^*}\nonumber\\
&=\pair{x_n-a_n}{a_n^*+L^*y_n^*}
+\pair{Lx_n-b_n}{b_n^*-y_n^*}\nonumber\\
&=\gamma_n^{-1}\pair{x_n-a_n}{\nabla h(x_n)
-\nabla h(a_n)}+\mu_n^{-1}
\pair{Lx_n-b_n}{\nabla j(Lx_n)-\nabla j(b_n)}
\nonumber\\
&\ensuremath{\geqslant}\sigma^{-1}\big(D^h(x_n,a_n)+D^h(a_n,x_n)
+D^j(Lx_n,b_n)+D^j(b_n,Lx_n)\big)\nonumber\\
&\ensuremath{\geqslant}\sigma^{-1}\big(D^h(x_n,a_n)+D^j(Lx_n,b_n)\big).
\end{align}
However, since \ref{IVt:1iii} yields
\begin{equation}
\label{IVe:IV4}
D^f(x_{n+1/2},x_n)\to 0
\quad\text{and}\quad
D^{g^*}(y_{n+1/2}^*,y_n^*)\to 0
\end{equation}
and since $f$ and $g^*$ satisfy Condition~\ref{IVC:1}, 
\eqref{IVbcn5y713b} yields
\begin{equation}
\label{IVe:IV5}
x_{n+1/2}-x_n\to 0
\quad\text{and}\quad
y_{n+1/2}^*-y_n^*\to 0.
\end{equation}
Since $\nabla h$ is uniformly continuous on every bounded subset of 
$\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} h$, Lemma~\ref{IVlKKnhf74g10} asserts that $\nabla h$ 
is bounded on every bounded subset of $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} h$ and hence, 
since $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int}\,}\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,} f\subset\ensuremath{\text{\rm int}\,}\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,} h$ and $L^*$ is bounded, 
it follows from \eqref{eKKnhf74g15a} that
$\big(\nabla h(x_n)-\gamma_nL^*y_n^*\big)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}$ is bounded. 
We therefore deduce from \eqref{4:e12}, 
\eqref{IVe:Bfg+uh4f9-09h},
and Lemma~\ref{IVlKKnhf74g12} that
\begin{equation}
\label{4:e18}
(a_n)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}\in(\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} h)^{\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}\;\;\text{is bounded}.
\end{equation}
Similarly, since $\nabla j$ is uniformly continuous on every 
bounded subset 
of $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} j$ and $L(\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} f)\subset\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} j$, it follows 
from \eqref{eKKnhf74g15a} and Lemma~\ref{IVlKKnhf74g10} 
that $\big(\nabla j(Lx_n)+\mu_ny_n^*\big)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}$ is bounded 
and hence \eqref{4:e12}, \eqref{IVe:Bfg+uh4f9-09h},
and Lemma~\ref{IVlKKnhf74g12} yield
\begin{equation}
\label{4:e19}
(b_n)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}\in(\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} j)^{\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}\;\;\text{is bounded}.
\end{equation} 
Thus, $(\nabla h(x_n))_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}$, $(\nabla h(a_n))_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}$,
$(\nabla j(Lx_n))_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}$, and $(\nabla j(b_n))_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}$ 
are bounded and we deduce from \eqref{IVe:Bfg+uh4f9-09h} that 
\begin{equation}
\label{eKKnhf74g15b}
(a^*_n)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}\quad\text{and}\quad(b^*_n)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}
\quad\text{are bounded}.
\end{equation}
We therefore derive from 
\eqref{IVe:8re84}, \eqref{IVe:IV5}, \eqref{4:e18}, and 
\eqref{4:e19} that
\begin{equation}
\label{eKKnhf74g15h}
D^h(x_n,a_n)\to 0\quad\text{and}\quad
D^j(Lx_n,b_n)\to 0.
\end{equation}
Since $h$ and $j$ satisfy Condition~\ref{IVC:1}, we get
\begin{equation}
\label{IVe:IV10}
x_n-a_n\to 0\quad\text{and}\quad Lx_n-b_n\to 0.
\end{equation}
Therefore, since $\nabla h$ is uniformly continuous on every 
bounded subset of $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} h$ and $\nabla j$ is uniformly 
continuous on every bounded subset of $\ensuremath{\text{\rm int\,dom}\,} j$,
\begin{equation}
\label{IVe:IV12a}
\nabla h(x_n)-\nabla h(a_n)\to 0\quad\text{and}\quad
\nabla j(Lx_n)-\nabla j(b_n)\to 0.
\end{equation}
Hence, using \eqref{IVe:Bfg+uh4f9-09h}, we get
\begin{equation}
\label{IVe:IV13}
a_n^*+L^*y_n^*\to 0
\quad\text{and}\quad 
b_n^*-y_n^*\to 0.
\end{equation}
Now, let 
$\boldsymbol{x}=(x,y^*)\in\ensuremath{{\mathfrak W}}(\boldsymbol{x}_n)_{n\in\ensuremath{\mathbb N}}$,
say $\boldsymbol{x}_{k_n}\ensuremath{\:\rightharpoonup\:}\boldsymbol{x}$. 
Then $x_{k_n}\ensuremath{\:\rightharpoonup\:} x$ and $y_{k_n}^*\ensuremath{\:\rightharpoonup\:} y^*$, and we 
derive from \eqref{IVe:IV10} and \eqref{IVe:IV13} that
\begin{equation}
\label{IVe:IV11}
\begin{cases}
a_{k_n}\ensuremath{\:\rightharpoonup\:} x\\
b^*_{k_n}\ensuremath{\:\rightharpoonup\:} y^*
\end{cases}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\begin{cases}
La_{k_n}-b_{k_n}\to 0\\
a^*_{k_n}+L^*b^*_{k_n}\to 0.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
It therefore follows from \eqref{eKKnhf74g04},
Proposition~\ref{ppOlk28ha12}\ref{ppOlk28ha12iii},
and \eqref{eKKnhf74g15a} that
$\boldsymbol{x}\in\boldsymbol{Z}\cap\overline{\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,}}\boldsymbol{f}
=\boldsymbol{C}$. Hence, we derive from 
Proposition~\ref{p:2}\ref{p:2iv} that 
\begin{equation}
\label{eKKnhf74g15x}
D^{f}({x}_n,\overline{x})+D^{g^*}(y^*_n,\overline{y}^*)=
D^{\boldsymbol{f}}(\boldsymbol{x}_n,\overline{\boldsymbol{x}})\to 0,
\end{equation}
where $\overline{\boldsymbol{x}}=(\overline{x},\overline{y}^*)$.
Hence, $D^{f}({x}_n,\overline{x})\to 0$, 
$D^{g^*}(y^*_n,\overline{y}^*)\to 0$, and, 
since $f$ and $g^*$ satisfy Condition~\ref{IVC:1}, we conclude
that $x_n\to\overline{x}$ and $y^*_n\to\overline{y}^*$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
\label{IVrKKnhf74g31}
We provide a couple of settings that satisfy the assumptions of
Theorem~\ref{IVt:1}.
\begin{enumerate}
\item
In Problem~\ref{prob:1}, suppose that $\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}$ and $\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}$ are Hilbert 
spaces, that $f=\|\cdot\|^2/2$, and that $g=\|\cdot\|^2/2$.
Furthermore, in Theorem~\ref{IVt:1}, set $h=f$ and $j=g$, and note
that, for any $\varepsilon\in\ensuremath{\left]0,+\infty\right[}$, 
$\nabla h+\varepsilon A=\ensuremath{\operatorname{Id}}\,+\varepsilon A$ and 
$\nabla j+\varepsilon B=\ensuremath{\operatorname{Id}}\,+\varepsilon B$ are strongly monotone 
and hence coercive by Lemma~\ref{l:20}\ref{l:20ii}.
Then we recover the framework of \cite{Nfao15}, 
which has been applied to domain decomposition problems in 
\cite{Atto15}. 
\item
Let $(\Omega_1,\mathcal{F}_1,\mu_1)$ and
$(\Omega_2,\mathcal{F}_2,\mu_2)$ be measure spaces,
let $p$ and $q$ be in $\left]1,\ensuremath{{+\infty}}\right[$, and set
$p^*=p/(p-1)$ and $q^*=q/(q-1)$. In Problem~\ref{prob:1}, suppose 
that $\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}=L^p(\Omega_1,\mathcal{F}_1,\mu_1)$, 
$\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}=L^q(\Omega_2,\mathcal{F}_2,\mu_2)$, $f=\|\cdot\|^p/p$, 
and $g=\|\cdot\|^q/q$. Then
$\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}^*=L^{p^*}(\Omega_1,\mathcal{F}_1,\mu_1)$,
$\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}^*=L^{q^*}(\Omega_2,\mathcal{F}_2,\mu_2)$, and
$g^*=\|\cdot\|^{q^*}/q^*$. Moreover, 
it follows from Clarkson's theorem 
\cite[Theorem~II.4.7]{IVCi90_B} that $\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}$, $\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}^*$,
$\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}$, and $\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}^*$ are uniformly convex and uniformly smooth.
Hence, we derive from \cite[Corollary~5.5 and Example~6.5]{Ccm01} 
that $f$, $g$, and $g^*$ are Legendre functions which are 
uniformly convex on every bounded set, and which therefore 
satisfy Condition~\ref{IVC:1} by virtue of 
\cite[Example~4.10(i)]{Sico03}. 
Now set $h=f$ and $j=g$ in Theorem~\ref{IVt:1}.
We derive from 
\cite[Theorem~II.2.16(i)]{IVCi90_B} that $\nabla h$ and $\nabla j$ 
are uniformly continuous on every bounded subset of $\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}$ and 
$\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}$, respectively. 
In addition, $h$ and $j$ are supercoercive and therefore,
for any $\varepsilon\in\ensuremath{\left]0,+\infty\right[}$, 
Proposition~\ref{IVpp:20a}\ref{IVcl:20b4} asserts that 
$\nabla h+\varepsilon A$ and $\nabla j+\varepsilon B$ are 
coercive. Finally, it follows from 
\cite[Proposition~II.4.9]{IVCi90_B} that
$\nabla h\colon x\mapsto |x|^{p-1}\ensuremath{\text{\rm sign}}(x)$ and 
$\nabla j\colon y\mapsto |y|^{q-1}\ensuremath{\text{\rm sign}}(y)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The implementation of algorithm \eqref{IVe:Bfg+uh4f9-09h}
requires the evaluation of the operator $(\nabla h+A)^{-1}$.
We provide a simple example in the Euclidean plane $\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}$ of a
maximally monotone operator $A$ for which 
$(\nabla h+A)^{-1}$ can be computed explicitly, whereas
the classical resolvent $(\ensuremath{\operatorname{Id}}\,+A)^{-1}$ is difficult to evaluate.
Let $\beta\in\ensuremath{\left]0,+\infty\right[}$ and let $\psi\colon\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}\to\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ be a Legendre 
function with a $\beta$ Lipschitz-continuous derivative.
Set
\begin{equation}
A\colon\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^2\to\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^2\colon (\xi_1,\xi_2)\mapsto
\big(\beta\xi_1-\psi'(\xi_1)-\xi_2,
\xi_1+\beta\xi_2-\psi'(\xi_2)\big)
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
h\colon\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^2\to\ensuremath{\left]-\infty,+\infty\right]}\colon (\xi_1,\xi_2)\mapsto
\psi(\xi_1)+\psi(\xi_2).
\end{equation}
Then it follows from \cite[Theorem~18.15]{Livre1} that $A$ is the
sum of the gradient of the convex function 
$(\xi_1,\xi_2)\mapsto\beta\xi_1^2/2-\psi(\xi_1)+
\beta\xi_2^2/2-\psi(\xi_2)$ 
and of the skew linear operator $(\xi_1,\xi_2)\mapsto(-\xi_2,\xi_1)$.
Thus, $A$ is a maximally monotone operator 
\cite[Corollary~24.4]{Livre1} which is not the
subdifferential of a convex function. In addition, as in 
Proposition~\ref{pvb8uh4504}\ref{pvb8uh4504i},
$h$ is a Legendre function and
\begin{equation}
\big(\forall (\xi_1,\xi_2)\in\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^2\big)\quad 
(\nabla h+A)^{-1}(\xi_1,\xi_2)
=\Bigg(\dfrac{\beta\xi_1+\xi_2}{1+\beta^2},
\dfrac{\beta\xi_2-\xi_1}{1+\beta^2}\Bigg).
\end{equation}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
\label{rvb8uh4504}
At every iteration $n$, algorithm \eqref{IVe:Bfg+uh4f9-09h}
requires the computation of $\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}=
P_{\boldsymbol{H}_n}^{\boldsymbol{f}}(x_n,y_n^*)$ and then of
$\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1}=Q^{\boldsymbol{f}}((x_0,y_0^*),(x_n,y_n^*),
(x_{n+1/2},y_{n+1/2}^*))$. 
Set $\boldsymbol{s}_n^*=(a_n^*+L^*b_n^*,b_n-La_n)$, 
$\eta_n=\pair{a_n}{a_n^*}+\pair{b_n}{b_n^*}$, and
$\boldsymbol{x}_n=(x_n,y_n^*)$,
Then, if $\boldsymbol{x}_n\not\in\boldsymbol{H}_n$, 
$\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}$ is the Bregman projection of 
$\boldsymbol{x}_n$ onto the closed affine 
hyperplane $\menge{\boldsymbol{x}\in\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathcal X}}}
{\pair{\boldsymbol{x}}{\boldsymbol{s}_n^*}=\eta_n}$. Thus,
$\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}$ is the solution of the problem 
\begin{equation}
\label{evb8uh4505a}
\minimize{\pair{\boldsymbol{p}}{\boldsymbol{s}_n^*}=\eta_n}
{\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{p})-\pair{\boldsymbol{p}}{\nabla
\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_n)}}
\end{equation}
which, using standard first order conditions, is characterized by
(see also \cite[Remark~6.13]{BB97} and 
\cite[Lemma~2.2.1]{Cens97}) 
\begin{equation}
\label{evb8uh4505b}
\begin{cases}
\nabla\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2})=\nabla
\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_n)-\lambda\boldsymbol{s}_n^*\\
\pair{\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}}{\boldsymbol{s}_n^*}=\eta_n\\
\lambda\in\ensuremath{\left]0,+\infty\right[}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
In view of \cite[Theorem~5.10]{Ccm01}, the Lagrange multiplier 
$\lambda$ is uniquely determined by the equation 
$\pair{\nabla\boldsymbol{f}^*(\nabla\boldsymbol{f}
(\boldsymbol{x}_n)-\lambda\boldsymbol{s}_n^*)}
{\boldsymbol{s}_n^*}=\eta_n$. The problem therefore reduces to
finding the solution $\overline{\lambda}$ to this equation in $\ensuremath{\left]0,+\infty\right[}$
and then setting $\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}=\nabla\boldsymbol{f}^*(
\nabla\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_n)-\overline{\lambda}
\boldsymbol{s}_n^*)$. Likewise, it follows from \eqref{e:Q} that 
$\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1}$ is the unique solution to the problem 
\begin{equation}
\label{evb8uh4505c}
\minimize{\substack{\pair{\boldsymbol{p}-\boldsymbol{x}_n}
{\nabla\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_0)-
\nabla\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_n)}\ensuremath{\leqslant} 0\\
\pair{\boldsymbol{p}-\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2}}
{\nabla\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n})-
\nabla\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1/2})}\ensuremath{\leqslant} 0}}
{\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{p})-\pair{\boldsymbol{p}}{\nabla
\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x}_0)}}.
\end{equation}
Depending on the number of active constraints, this 
problem boils down to determining 
up to two Lagrange multipliers in $\ensuremath{\left]0,+\infty\right[}$.
\end{remark}

Next, we consider a specialization of Problem~\ref{prob:1} to
multivariate structured minimization.

\begin{problem}
\label{IVpb:V3}
Let $m$ and $p$ be strictly positive integers, let
$(\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}_i)_{1\ensuremath{\leqslant} i\ensuremath{\leqslant} m}$ and $(\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}_k)_{1\ensuremath{\leqslant} k\ensuremath{\leqslant} p}$ be 
reflexive real Banach spaces, and let 
$\boldsymbol{\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}}$ be the standard vector product space 
$\Big(\ensuremath{\raisebox{-0.5mm}{\mbox{\huge{$\times$}}}}_{\!\!i=1}^{\!\!m}\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}_i\Big)\times
\Big(\ensuremath{\raisebox{-0.5mm}{\mbox{\huge{$\times$}}}}_{\!\!k=1}^{\!\!p}\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}_k^*\Big)$ 
equipped with the norm 
\begin{equation}
(x,y^*)=\big((x_i)_{1\ensuremath{\leqslant} i\ensuremath{\leqslant} m},(y_k^*)_{1\ensuremath{\leqslant} k\ensuremath{\leqslant} p}\big)
\mapsto\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^m\|x_i\|^2+\sum_{k=1}^p\|y_k^*\|^2}.
\end{equation} 
For every $i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}$ and every $k\in\{1,\ldots,p\}$, 
let $\varphi_i\in\Gamma_0(\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}_i)$, let $\psi_k\in\Gamma_0(\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}_k)$,
and let $L_{ki}\colon\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}_i\to\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}_k$ be linear and bounded. 
Consider the primal problem 
\begin{equation}
\label{4eKKnhf74g201}
\minimize{x_1\in\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}_1,\ldots,\,x_m\in\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}_m}{\sum_{i=1}^m
\varphi_i(x_i)+\sum_{k=1}^p 
\psi_k\bigg(\sum_{i=1}^mL_{ki}x_i\bigg)},
\end{equation}
the dual problem
\begin{equation}
\label{4eKKnhf74g202}
\minimize{y^*_1\in\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}_1^*,\ldots,\,y^*_p\in\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}_p^*}{\sum_{i=1}^m
\varphi_i^*\bigg(-\sum_{k=1}^pL_{ki}^*y^*_k\bigg)
+\sum_{k=1}^p\psi^*_k(y^*_k)}, 
\end{equation}
and let 
\begin{multline}
\boldsymbol{Z}=\bigg\{({x},{y}^*)
\in\boldsymbol{\mathcal{X}}\;\bigg |\;
(\forall i\in\{1,\ldots,m\})\;\;-\sum_{k=1}^pL_{ki}^*y_k^*
\in\partial\varphi_i(x_i)\:\;\text{and}\\
(\forall k\in\{1,\ldots,p\})\;\;\sum_{i=1}^mL_{ki}x_i\in
\partial\psi_k^*(y_k^*)\bigg\}
\end{multline}
be the associated Kuhn-Tucker set. For every $i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}$,
let $f_i\in\Gamma_0(\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}_i)$ be a Legendre function and let
$x_{i,0}\in\ensuremath{\text{\rm int}\,}\ensuremath{\text{\rm dom}\,} f_i$. For every $k\in\{1,\ldots,p\}$, 
let $g_k\in\Gamma_0(\ensuremath{{\mathcal Y}}_k)$ be a Legendre function and let
